WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:08.390
AMY GOODMAN: As we continue to talk about
the latest shake-up in the Trump administration,

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:13.559
let’s bring into this conversation former
CIA officer John Kiriakou, who personally

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:18.619
knew CIA director nominee Gina Haspel when
he worked at the CIA.

00:00:18.619 --> 00:00:23.079
But their careers have taken very different
paths over the past decade.

00:00:23.079 --> 00:00:28.579
Haspel, who was directly involved in torture
at a secret CIA prison in Thailand, has been

00:00:28.579 --> 00:00:31.369
promoted to now head the agency.

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:37.120
Kiriakou, who blew the whistle on the torture
program, ended up being jailed for almost

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.489
two years.

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:41.250
John Kiriakou, welcome back to Democracy Now!

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:48.590
Can you respond to President Trump’s decision
to fire Rex Tillerson and replace Tillerson

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:50.670
with Pompeo as secretary of state?

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:55.999
Pompeo, head of the CIA, would be then replaced
by the deputy director of the CIA, Gina Haspel.

00:00:55.999 --> 00:01:01.710
JOHN KIRIAKOU: Well, so many of us believed
a year ago that Rex Tillerson was going to

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.000
be the adult in the room at the White House.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.860
That turned out to be the case, in a way,
but he was so disrespected by this president

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.830
and those around him that he was probably
doomed from the beginning.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:21.110
On Mike Pompeo, I’ve said from the beginning,
you can disagree with him politically and

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:27.270
ideologically, but he doesn’t have the kind
of baggage that Gina Haspel has.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:32.300
I wouldn’t have chosen Mike Pompeo to be
the secretary of state, but, I guess, in the

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.350
greater scheme of things, we could have done
worse.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:39.350
Gina Haspel, however, is a problem, a big
problem.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.130
I’ve been talking about Gina Haspel for
more than a year.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:46.660
She was a dreadful choice to be the deputy
director.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.250
She’s a worse choice to be the director.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:54.521
I think it’s wonderful that there’s a
possibility of a woman heading the CIA, but

00:01:54.521 --> 00:01:59.100
there are 50 different women who are qualified
to lead the CIA.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.160
It shouldn’t be Gina Haspel.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:06.180
AMY GOODMAN: So talk about why you and others
called her “Bloody Gina.”

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.000
JOHN KIRIAKOU: We did call her Bloody Gina.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.950
Gina was always very quick and very willing
to use force.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:21.160
You know, there was a group of officers in
the CIA’s Counterterrorism Center, when

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:29.020
I was—when I was serving there, who—I
hate to even make the accusation out loud,

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.080
but I’m going to say it: who enjoyed using
force.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.720
Yeah, everybody knew that torture didn’t
work.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:35.890
That’s not even the issue.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.270
Lots of different things work.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:41.130
Was it moral, and was it ethical, and was
it legal?

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.630
I think the answers to those questions are
very clearly no.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:49.950
But Gina and people like Gina did it, I think,
because they enjoyed doing it.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:54.610
They tortured just for the sake of torture,
not for the sake of gathering information.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:03:03.510
AMY GOODMAN: And can you talk more specifically
about Gina Haspel heading up this secret CIA

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:09.260
black site in Thailand and—for those who
aren’t familiar with even what a black site

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:15.660
is—and the fact that she not only headed
it up, but was directly involved with the

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:16.660
torture?

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:22.640
JOHN KIRIAKOU: First, let me preface any comments
that I make by saying that the CIA’s Publications

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:28.520
Review Board is specifically prohibiting me
from talking about Gina Haspel’s résumé,

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.680
especially her overseas duty.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:38.050
With that said, the media has reported extensively
on what Gina Haspel has done over the course

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.630
of her career, so I’ll leave it at that.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:48.300
With that said, a black site is a site that’s
more or less off the books, meaning it doesn’t

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:49.760
officially exist.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.290
It exists, but nobody is supposed to know
that it exists.

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:57.500
And in many cases, that includes the congressional
oversight committees.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:04.210
So, in the immediate aftermath of 9/11, the
CIA set up such black sites all around the

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:10.380
world, with the idea being that if we captured
someone from al-Qaeda or kidnapped someone

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:15.709
from al-Qaeda, we could send him to one of
these black sites, interrogate him without

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:23.699
having to worry about the law, about ethics
or morality or the FBI breathing down their

00:04:23.699 --> 00:04:24.729
backs.

00:04:24.729 --> 00:04:26.449
They could do whatever they wanted.

00:04:26.449 --> 00:04:30.580
And that’s how this torture program spiraled
out of control.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.569
There were people who were murdered in the
course of their interrogations.

